<title>UK: UK retail sales up 0.5 pct in Feb.</title>
<headline>UK retail sales up 0.5 pct in Feb.</headline>
<dateline>LONDON 1997-03-19</dateline>
<text>
<p>The Office for National Statistics released the following seasonally adjusted data on UK retail sales in February.</p>
<p>    RETAIL SALES VOLUME    FEB     JAN   (PREL JAN)</p>
<p>    Monthly s/adj change   0.5     0.7	(0.6)</p>
<p>    Yr-on-yr change	  4.4     4.8	(4.6)</p>
<p>    Index (base 1990=100)  113.5   112.9  (112.7)</p>
<p>Market forecasts had centred on a 0.1 percent rise in retail sales on the month and a year-on-year increase of 3.6 percent.  </p>
<p>    3m/3m			0.6     n/a     (0.9)</p>
<p>    3m/3m yr ago	     4.0     n/a     (3.9)</p>
<p>BY SECTOR (percentage gain, minus indicates fall)</p>
<p>			    m/m	y/y	3m/3m    3m/3m yr ago</p>
<p>Predominantly food    unch     2.7	1.0	   3.0 total</p>
<p>pred non-food	   1.4	7.5	1.0	   5.9</p>
<p>non-specialised	 1.2	7.1	2.1	   7.4</p>
<p>Textile,clothing,foot 1.4	5.6     -1.1	   4.6</p>
<p>Household goods	 2.1     10.8	2.8	   9.5 Other</p>
<p>stores		    0.8	6.5	1.0	   3.3</p>
<p>non-store retailing</p>
<p>  and repair	   -3.0     -7.7     -4.2	  -3.3</p>
<p>The average weekly non-seasonally adjusted value of retail sales in February was 3.070 billion stg, up 4.7 percent on February 1996.</p>
<p>In value terms, retail sales rose by 5.0 percent in the three months to end February on the same period a year earlier.</p>
</text>
